\use{document}
\use{music}

\title{The \texttt{music} Package}
\author{Simple staff notation, drawn from the document language}
\maketitle

\section{Reading a score}

The \texttt{music} package draws a line of staff notation on a treble clef. A score is
a list of notes: a letter \texttt{a}–\texttt{g} for the pitch, optional \texttt{'} or
\texttt{,} to raise or lower it an octave, and an optional duration digit — \texttt{1}
whole, \texttt{2} half, \texttt{4} quarter (the default), \texttt{8} eighth. A \texttt{|}
is a barline. Note heads, stems, flags and ledger lines are all drawn over the
\texttt{\\picture} layer; there is no music-specific engine code.

\section{A scale}

The C major scale, an octave of quarter notes from middle C — which sits on its own
ledger line below the staff — up to the C above.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\section{Durations}

The same C, held for a whole note, a half, a quarter, and an eighth. The longer notes
have open heads; the stem appears at the half note, and the flag at the eighth.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c1 c2 c4 c8}}

\section{A melody}

The opening of \emph{Mary Had a Little Lamb}, with barlines and a half note to close
each phrase.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{e d c d | e e e2 | d d d2 | e g g2}}

\bigskip
And the start of \emph{Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star}.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c c g g | a a g2 | f f e e | d d c2}}

\section{Range and ledger lines}

Notes far above or below the staff hang on short ledger lines, drawn only as far as the
note reaches.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c, e, g, c g c' e' g'}}
